 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **[Self-hosted WebSocket delivery](decisions/websocket-delivery.md).** Connected browsers receive committed revisions through application-operated WebSocket sessions. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it adds avoidable update lag and repeated reads; a hosted realtime service was rejected because it couples this core board capability to a provider and recurring vendor cost. This choice requires the application to operate connection capacity, reconnect handling, reliable publication, and revision replay.
